window.docContents[116].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Unit-in-an-associative-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Unit-in-an-associative-algebra--Unit-in-an-associative-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary predicate on elements of a module equipped with the referenced auxiliary structure.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Definition2.2.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Discussion_2.1_overview/Derived2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.2. Algebras\u0009Unit in an associative algebra\u0009Unit in an associative algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___2___-Algebras/Unit-in-an-associative-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___2___-Algebras--Unit-in-an-associative-algebra--Unit-in-an-associative-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4/#Chapter3___Discussion_after_Lemma3___1___6":{"contents":"Now we are ready to prove Proposition 3.1.4. Let W be a submodule of V := \\bigoplus_X V_X \\otimes X, where \\dim V < \\infty. We claim that W = \\bigoplus_X W_X \\otimes X for some vector spaces W_X \\subseteq V_X. Indeed, by Lemma 3.1.6 have V/W = \\bigoplus_X U_X \\otimes X for some vector spaces U_X, so\n\nW = \\operatorname{Ker}(V \\to V/W) = \\bigoplus_X \\operatorname{Ker}(V_X \\to U_X) \\otimes X,\n\nas desired.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.1. Subrepresentations in semisimple representations\u0009Completion of alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4","header":"Completion of alternative proof of Proposition 3.1.4","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___1___-Subrepresentations-in-semisimple-representations/Completion-of-alternative-proof-of-Proposition-3___1___4/#Chapter3___Discussion_after_Lemma3___1___6"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Equivalent-characterizations-of-semisimple-algebras/#Chapter3___Proposition3___5___8":{"contents":"Proposition 3.5.8. For a finite dimensional algebra A, the following are equivalent:\n\n(1) A is semisimple.\n\n(2) \\sum_i (\\dim V_i)^2 = \\dim A, where the V_i's are the irreducible representations of A.\n\n(3) A \\cong \\bigoplus_i \\operatorname{Mat}_{d_i}(k) for some d_i.\n\n(4) Any finite dimensional representation of A is completely reducible (that is, isomorphic to a direct sum of irreducible representations).\n\n(5) A is a completely reducible representation of A.\n\nProof. As \\dim A - \\dim \\operatorname{Rad}(A) = \\sum_i (\\dim V_i)^2, clearly \\dim A = \\sum_i (\\dim V_i)^2 if and only if \\operatorname{Rad}(A) = 0. Thus, (1) \\Leftrightarrow (2).\n\nBy Theorem 3.5.4, if \\operatorname{Rad}(A) = 0, then clearly A \\cong \\bigoplus_i \\operatorname{Mat}_{d_i}(k) for d_i = \\dim V_i. Thus, (1) \\Rightarrow (3).\n\nNext, (3) \\Rightarrow (4) by Theorem 3.3.1. Clearly (4) \\Rightarrow (5).\n\nTo see that (5) \\Rightarrow (1), note that if A is a completely reducible representation of A, then each element of \\operatorname{Rad}(A) kills it, but the only element that kills 1 \\in A is 0; thus \\operatorname{Rad}(A) = 0, so A is semisimple. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.5. Finite dimensional algebras\u0009Equivalent characterizations of semisimple algebras","header":"Equivalent characterizations of semisimple algebras","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___5___-Finite-dimensional-algebras/Equivalent-characterizations-of-semisimple-algebras/#Chapter3___Proposition3___5___8"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Section-4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___9":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.9. Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables\u0009Section 4.9: Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables","header":"Section 4.9: Computing tensor product multiplicities using character tables","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/Section-4___9___-Computing-tensor-product-multiplicities-using-character-tables/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___9"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials/#Chapter5___Remark5___15___2":{"contents":"Remark 5.15.2. Here is an equivalent formulation of Theorem 5.15.1: \\chi_{V_\\lambda}(C_\\mathbf{i}) is the coefficient of x^\\lambda in the (Laurent) polynomial\n\n\\prod_{i < j} \\left(1 - \\frac{x_j}{x_i}\\right) \\prod_{m \\geq 1} H_m(x)^{i_m}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.15. The Frobenius character formula\u0009Equivalent formulation of Theorem 5.15.1 using Laurent polynomials","header":"Equivalent formulation of Theorem 5.15.1 using Laurent polynomials","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Equivalent-formulation-of-Theorem-5___15___1-using-Laurent-polynomials/#Chapter5___Remark5___15___2"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___7___-Coxeter-elements/Lemma-6___7___2___-Coxeter-element-eventually-produces-a-negative-coefficient/#Chapter6___Lemma6___7___2":{"contents":"Lemma 6.7.2. Let\n\n\n\\beta = \\sum_i k_i \\alpha_i\n\n\nwith k_i \\geq 0 for all i but not all k_i = 0. Then there is n \\in \\mathbb{N}, such that\n\n\nc^N \\beta\n\n\nhas at least one strictly negative coefficient.\n\nProof. The Coxeter element c belongs to a finite group W. So there is M \\in \\mathbb{N}, such that\n\n\nc^M = 1.\n\n\nWe claim that\n\n\n1 + c + c^2 + \\cdots + c^{M-1} = 0\n\n\nas operators on \\mathbb{R}^n. This implies what we need, since \\beta has at least one strictly positive coefficient, so one of the elements\n\n\nc\\beta, c^2 \\beta, \\ldots, c^{M-1} \\beta\n\n\nmust have at least one strictly negative coefficient. Furthermore, it is enough to show that 1 is not an eigenvalue for c, since\n\n\n(1 + c + c^2 + \\ldots + c^{M-1})v = w \\neq 0\n\n\n\n\\implies cw = c\\left(1 + c + c^2 + \\cdots + c^{M-1}\\right)v\n\n\n\n= (c + c^2 + c^3 + \\cdots + c^{M-1} + 1)v = w.\n\nAssume the contrary, i.e., 1 is an eigenvalue of c and let v be a corresponding eigenvector:\n\n\ncv = v \\implies s_1 \\ldots s_n v = v \\iff s_2 \\ldots s_n v = s_1 v.\n\n\nBut since s_i only changes the ith coordinate of v, we get\n\n\ns_1 v = v \\quad \\text{and} \\quad s_2 \\ldots s_n v = v.\n\n\nRepeating the same procedure, we get\n\n\ns_i v = v\n\n\nfor all i. But this means\n\n\nB(v, \\alpha_i) = 0\n\n\nfor all i, and since B is nondegenerate, we get v = 0. But this is a contradiction, since v is an eigenvector. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.7. Coxeter elements\u0009Lemma 6.7.2: Coxeter element eventually produces a negative coefficient","header":"Lemma 6.7.2: Coxeter element eventually produces a negative coefficient","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___7___-Coxeter-elements/Lemma-6___7___2___-Coxeter-element-eventually-produces-a-negative-coefficient/#Chapter6___Lemma6___7___2"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories/#Chapter9___Theorem9___6___4":{"contents":"Theorem 9.6.4. The functor F is an equivalence of categories. Thus, any finite abelian category over a field k is equivalent to the category of modules over a finite dimensional k-algebra.\n\nThe proof of Theorem 9.6.4 is contained in the following problem.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories\u0009Finite abelian categories are module categories","header":"Finite abelian categories are module categories","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories/#Chapter9___Theorem9___6___4"}});